@startuml theme-change-sequence
!pragma teoz true
skinparam defaultFontName "Noto Sans"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
title A theme change: the chrome, its pages, the window's decorations and the settings file

actor "user" as User #FFF2CC
box "F1R3Gaze" #F4F8FC
  participant "ChromeDocument\n(chrome.rs)" as Chrome #DDEBF7
  participant "Profile::set_theme\n(profile.rs)" as Profile #DDEBF7
  participant "theme::resolve\n(theme.rs)" as Resolve #DDEBF7
  participant "ChromeApplication\n(application.rs)" as App #DDEBF7
end box
box "Blitz and winit" #F5FAF2
  participant "the chrome's document\nand its pages (blitz-dom)" as Doc #E2EFDA
  participant "View and Window\n(blitz-shell, winit)" as Window #E2EFDA
end box
box "The system" #F5FAF2
  participant "XDG portal\n(dbus-send)" as Portal #E2EFDA
end box
participant "<config>/settings.toml" as Settings #FCE4D6

== The user chooses Light in Appearance ==
User -> Chrome : click Light (data-action="theme:light")
Chrome -> Chrome : Action::parse: Theme(ThemeOp::Light)\n(next, template and custom are refused)
Chrome -> Profile : set_theme(BuiltIn(Light))
Profile -> Profile : the session's choice first
Profile -> Settings : [appearance] theme = "default-light"\n(write_setting: comments kept, written atomically)
note right of Settings #F8CBAD
  Refused in a read-only session:
  "The theme applies to this session only"
end note
Chrome -> Resolve : resolve(choice, system's preference, fs, theme folders)
Resolve --> Chrome : Resolved { colours, scheme: Light, file, problem }
Chrome -> Doc : paint_theme: --gaze-* on <html>;\nthe host sheet of every built-in page;\nviewport.color_scheme = Light (L13)
Chrome -> Chrome : request(Scheme { Light, follows_system: false })\n(only the latest Scheme is kept)
App -> Chrome : take_window_requests()\n(after the event, and once per loop turn)
App -> Window : set_theme_override(Some(Light)), if it changes:\nthe window's own theme no longer replaces the scheme
App -> Window : set_theme(decoration_theme(platform, Light, false)) = Some(Light),\nif it changes
Doc -> Doc : next layout: resolve copies color_scheme into every\npage's viewport (resolve.rs), and each page restyles once
note right of Doc #FFF2CC
  Pages see prefers-color-scheme: light. A page that
  declares no color-scheme keeps light system colours
  in either scheme (PAGE_SCHEME_CSS, L13 H2).
end note

== The system turns dark while System is chosen (Linux) ==
Window -> App : WindowEvent::Focused(true)
App -> Chrome : window_focused(now)
Chrome -> Portal : refresh: dbus-send ... Settings.ReadOne color-scheme\n(a gaze-system-theme thread; at most every 2 s; killed at 1 s)
Portal --> Chrome : variant uint32 1 (dark); the window is woken
Chrome -> Chrome : poll: follow_system: the preference changed,\nso apply_theme resolves and paints again
Chrome -> Chrome : request(Scheme { Dark, follows_system: true })
App -> Window : set_theme_override(Some(Dark)); set_theme(Some(Dark))\n(X11 and Wayland report nothing themselves)
note over App, Window #DDEBF7
  macOS and Windows report the change instead: ThemeChanged
  goes to window_reported_scheme, and the window, which has no
  theme of its own while it follows the system, needs none.
  After every report the scheme is asked for again, and the
  decorations given are forgotten: Windows re-themes a window
  created without a theme at each settings change.
end note

== The user chooses System again ==
User -> Chrome : click System (theme:system)
Chrome -> Profile : set_theme(System)
Profile -> Settings : [appearance] theme = "system"
Chrome -> Portal : refresh(now): Linux asks again
Chrome -> Chrome : request(Scheme { shown, true }), request(FollowSystem)
App -> Window : macOS and Windows: set_theme(None),\nthe window follows the system again
App -> Window : event_loop.system_theme()\n(macOS reported nothing while the window had a theme)
App -> Chrome : window_reported_scheme(preference)
Chrome -> Chrome : follow_system; request(Scheme { preference, true })

legend right
  |= Colour |= Meaning |
  |<#DDEBF7> | F1R3Gaze code |
  |<#E2EFDA> | Blitz, winit and the system |
  |<#FFF2CC> | what the user does and sees |
  |<#FCE4D6> | the settings file |
  |<#F8CBAD> | a failure, and what happens then |
endlegend
@enduml
